      "id": 1,
      "text": "Ashland City was incorporated in 1859 and named for the local ash trees or Henry Clay’s Kentucky estate.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Ashland_City,_Tennessee",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Cheatham County was created in 1856, and Ashland City became its county seat shortly thereafter.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashland_City%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Cheatham Lake, a large recreational area on the Cumberland River, stretches past Ashland City and is managed by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashland_City%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s courthouse, built in 1869, replaced a temporary structure and now stands as a historic landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashland_City%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Ashland City sits at the crossroads of State Routes 12 and 49, linking Nashville, Clarksville, Springfield, and Kentucky.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
